\chapter{\texorpdfstring{The
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} Type
and Monadic
Operations}{The  Type and Monadic Operations}}\label{ch20-the-maybet-type-and-monadic-operations}

MuDSL ships a small, self-contained monadic optional called
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}. It
lives in the \texttt{dsl} namespace alongside the free-function
combinators \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}flat\_\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}filter}, and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else} that
operate on any optional-like value. The type is a thin value wrapper
around
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}:
it borrows the optional's storage, copy and move semantics, and
value-access primitives, then layers fluent combinators on top. The
result is a value type that can be chained through pure transformations
without the noise of repeated \texttt{if} checks.

This chapter describes the
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} API
in full: its constructors, accessors, and the four combinators
\texttt{map}, \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}, \texttt{filter}, and
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else}. It then builds up to realistic pipelines
that combine the combinators, and contrasts
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} with
the related free functions covered in Chapter 21 and the error-carrying
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
of Chapter 22. The chapter assumes familiarity with
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} and with value-semantic
C++20 templates.

\section{Motivation: Optional Values Without Branching
Noise}\label{ch20-motivation-optional-values-without-branching-noise}

Optional values are ubiquitous in DSL tooling. A lookup may fail, a
parse may produce no token, a rewrite rule may not apply, and a memoized
computation (Chapter 18) may not yet have a cached entry. The
standard-library answer is
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
which carries a value or none. The problem is not the representation but
the ergonomics: each transformation on an optional demands an explicit
check, a dereference, and a re-wrap.

When only one transformation is in play, the boilerplate is tolerable.
When several transformations compose, the code degenerates into a
staircase of nested \texttt{if} statements or temporary variables. The
intent of the computation --- ``apply this, then this, then this, but
stop if any step is empty'' --- is buried under plumbing.
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
exists to make that intent visible.

The combinators on
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} are
the same ones functional programmers recognize as functor \texttt{map},
monadic \texttt{bind} (here called \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}), and
the filtering step common to monad libraries. They are not exotic; they
are the minimal set needed to express a short-circuiting pipeline of
pure functions. By returning a fresh \texttt{Maybe} at each step, the
combinators compose left to right as a fluent chain.

This approach complements, rather than replaces, the lazy abstractions
of Chapter 19.
\texttt{Lazy\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} defers
a single computation;
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
models a computation that may produce no value. The two compose
naturally --- a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}Lazy\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is a deferred computation that may turn out to be unavailable --- but
they solve different problems and are not interchangeable.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} Class
Template}{The  Class Template}}\label{ch20-the-maybet-class-template}

\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is a
class template with a single type parameter \texttt{T}, the contained
value type. Its storage is exactly one
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
exposed through
\texttt{as\_\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} but
otherwise private. Because the storage is an optional, the layout,
alignment, and triviality of
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} track
those of
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.

The class is declared in the \texttt{dsl} namespace. Its documentation
comment states the design intent plainly: it wraps
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} semantics and adds
fluent
\texttt{map}/\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}/\texttt{filter}/\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else}.
The comment also notes that the monad laws --- left identity, right
identity, and associativity --- hold when the functions passed to
\texttt{map} and \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} are pure and
side-effect free. This is the standard caveat for any functor or monad:
the library enforces types, not purity.

The public surface is deliberately small. There are constructors, three
accessors (\texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}value},
\texttt{as\_\allowbreak{}optional}, and \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else}),
and three templated combinators (\texttt{map},
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}, \texttt{filter}). There is no
\texttt{operator*}, no \texttt{operator-\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
and no \texttt{operator bool}. Access to the contained value is meant to
go through the combinators or through
\texttt{as\_\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} when raw
optional access is genuinely required. This keeps the type's vocabulary
tight and discourages ad-hoc dereference.

The verbatim declaration shows the shape of the type:

\begin{code}
template <typename T> class Maybe
{
public:
  /// Constructs empty Maybe.
  Maybe () = default;
  /// Constructs Maybe with a value.
  Maybe (T value) : value_ (std::move (value)) {}
  /// Constructs Maybe from std::optional.
  Maybe (std::optional<T> value) : value_ (std::move (value)) {}
  // ... accessors and combinators ...
private:
  std::optional<T> value_{};
};
\end{code}

The defaulted default constructor produces an empty \texttt{Maybe}. The
value constructor moves (or copies) a \texttt{T} into the underlying
optional. The
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
constructor lifts an existing optional into the wrapper without copying
the optional's value more than necessary. Together these three
constructors cover every construction path an application needs.

\section{Construction}\label{ch20-construction}

Constructing a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is no
more complicated than constructing a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The empty form is
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\{\}},
which value-initializes the underlying optional to
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}nullopt}. The valued form is
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}x)\allowbreak{}},
where \texttt{x} is convertible to \texttt{T}. The third form,
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}opt)\allowbreak{}},
takes a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and is useful when interoperating with code that already produces
optionals.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <string>

dsl::Maybe<int> empty{};                 // no value
dsl::Maybe<int> valued(42);              // contains 42
dsl::Maybe<std::string> from_opt(std::optional<std::string>{"hi"});
\end{code}

The empty form is the identity element for the combinators: any
\texttt{map}, \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}, or \texttt{filter}
applied to an empty \texttt{Maybe} returns an empty \texttt{Maybe} of
the appropriate result type. This short-circuit behavior is what makes
chaining safe. A pipeline never has to test for emptiness between steps;
the combinators propagate it automatically.

Because the constructors take their argument by value and move it into
storage, constructing a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
from a string literal goes through one
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} construction and one
move. There is no surprising copy. For move-only types such as
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}unique\_\allowbreak{}ptr\textless{}\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
the value constructor accepts an rvalue and moves it in; the copy
constructor of
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
itself is implicitly deleted when the underlying optional is
non-copyable, which is the expected behavior.

The
\texttt{Maybe(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
constructor is the bridge to the rest of the standard library. Any
function that returns
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
can be absorbed into a \texttt{Maybe} pipeline by wrapping its result.
This is important in practice because MuDSL's free-function combinators
(Chapter 21) operate on raw optionals, and converting between the two
representations is a one-liner.

\section{Value Access}\label{ch20-value-access}

Three members give access to the contained value or its absence.
\texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} reports
whether a value is present.
\texttt{as\_\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns a
const reference to the underlying
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
which is useful when handing the value off to a generic optional
consumer.
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else(\allowbreak{}fallback)\allowbreak{}}
returns the contained value if present, otherwise the supplied fallback.

The \texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} method
is \texttt{const noexcept} and forwards directly to
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}has\_\allowbreak{}value}.
It is the predicate to use when a binary decision must be made at the
boundary of a pipeline --- for instance, to choose between logging a
warning and proceeding with a result.

\begin{code}
dsl::Maybe<int> m(7);
if (m.has_value()) {
  // proceed
}
\end{code}

The \texttt{as\_\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
accessor is the escape hatch. It returns
\texttt{const std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\&},
exposing the wrapped optional without copying. This is the recommended
way to interoperate with code that expects a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}, including the
free-function combinators described in Chapter 21. It is also the way to
obtain a reference for \texttt{*} and
\texttt{-\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} dereference when raw access is
unavoidable.

The \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} member is the terminating accessor.
It takes a fallback of type \texttt{T} and returns \texttt{T} by value,
using
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}value\_\allowbreak{}or}
internally. It is the standard way to collapse a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} back
into a plain \texttt{T} at the end of a pipeline. The fallback is moved
into the return value when the \texttt{Maybe} is empty, so passing an
rvalue fallback is cheap.

\begin{code}
int n = dsl::Maybe<int>{}.or_else(-1);   // n == -1
int m = dsl::Maybe<int>(8).or_else(-1);  // m == 8
\end{code}

There is no throwing accessor on
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
itself. Code that needs
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}'s throwing
\texttt{value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} should call
\texttt{as\_\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
This is a deliberate narrowing of the surface: the combinators are the
preferred way to consume a \texttt{Maybe}, and a throwing accessor would
tempt callers back into the branching style the type is meant to
replace.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{map}
Combinator}{The  Combinator}}\label{ch20-the-map-combinator}

\texttt{map} is the functor operation: it applies a function to the
contained value, if any, and returns a \texttt{Maybe} of the result
type. If the \texttt{Maybe} is empty, \texttt{map} returns an empty
\texttt{Maybe} of the result type without calling the function. The
function is therefore never invoked on absence, which is the property
that makes \texttt{map} safe to chain.

The signature, quoted verbatim from the header, is:

\begin{code}
/// Applies function to contained value, if present.
/// Applies function returning Maybe-like type, if value is present.
template <typename Fn>
auto
map (Fn &&fn) const -> Maybe<decltype (fn (std::declval<T> ()))>
{
  using U = decltype (fn (*value_));
  if (!value_)
    return Maybe<U>{};
  return Maybe<U>{ fn (*value_) };
}
\end{code}

The return type is deduced as
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}decltype(\allowbreak{}fn(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}declval\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
which is
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} where
\texttt{U} is the result of applying \texttt{fn} to a \texttt{T}. This
means the function passed to \texttt{map} must accept \texttt{T} (or a
value convertible from \texttt{T}), not
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Passing a function that itself returns a \texttt{Maybe} is legal but
produces a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
which is rarely what is wanted; \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} exists
for that case.

The body is straightforward. An empty optional yields an empty
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}. A
populated optional yields
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\{ fn(\allowbreak{}*value\_\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} \}}.
The function is called exactly once, on the dereferenced value, only
when a value is present. Note that \texttt{map} is a \texttt{const}
member: it does not mutate the receiver, it produces a fresh
\texttt{Maybe}.

A simple example increments an integer:

\begin{code}
auto r = dsl::Maybe<int>(4).map([](int x){ return x + 1; });
// r is Maybe<int>(5)
\end{code}

Because the return type is derived from the function, \texttt{map} can
change the contained type. Mapping a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
with a function returning \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}
produces a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
This is essential for pipelines that transform a value through several
types --- for instance, parsing an integer from a string and then
formatting it back.

\begin{code}
auto s = dsl::Maybe<int>(42)
           .map([](int x){ return std::to_string(x); })
           .map([](const std::string& s){ return s + "!"; });
// s is Maybe<std::string>("42!")
\end{code}

The empty case propagates silently. If the initial \texttt{Maybe} is
empty, neither lambda is called and the result is an empty
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
This is the short-circuit behavior in its simplest form: a missing input
yields a missing output, with no branching in user code.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}
Combinator}{The  Combinator}}\label{ch20-the-flat_map-combinator}

\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} is the monadic bind operation. It takes
a function that itself returns a \texttt{Maybe} (or any type
constructible from \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}nullopt}-like
emptiness) and flattens the result so that no nesting occurs. Where
\texttt{map} would produce
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} produces
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}. This
is the combinator for chaining steps that can themselves fail.

The verbatim definition is:

\begin{code}
template <typename Fn>
auto
flat_map (Fn &&fn) const -> decltype (fn (std::declval<T> ()))
{
  using R = decltype (fn (*value_));
  if (!value_)
    return R{};
  return fn (*value_);
}
\end{code}

The return type is
\texttt{decltype(\allowbreak{}fn(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}declval\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
i.e.~the exact type returned by \texttt{fn}. The body returns a
default-constructed \texttt{R} (which, for
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}, is
an empty
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}) when
the input is empty, and otherwise returns
\texttt{fn(\allowbreak{}*value\_\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} directly.
Because \texttt{fn} returns a \texttt{Maybe}, the result is already
flat.

The function passed to \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} must accept
\texttt{T} and return a \texttt{Maybe}. This is the difference from
\texttt{map}: \texttt{map}'s function returns a plain value,
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}'s returns a \texttt{Maybe}. Choosing the
wrong combinator is the most common mistake with this type, and it is
covered explicitly in the pitfalls section below.

A typical use is a lookup that may fail. Suppose a function
\texttt{find\_\allowbreak{}user(\allowbreak{}int id)\allowbreak{}}
returns
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}User\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Chaining a second lookup that fetches an email from a \texttt{User}
requires \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}, because the second lookup also
returns a \texttt{Maybe}:

\begin{code}
dsl::Maybe<Email> email =
  find_user(id)
    .flat_map([](const User& u){ return find_email(u); });
\end{code}

If \texttt{find\_\allowbreak{}user} returns empty,
\texttt{find\_\allowbreak{}email} is never called and the result is an
empty
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}Email\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
If \texttt{find\_\allowbreak{}user} returns a user,
\texttt{find\_\allowbreak{}email} is called and its result --- whether
valued or empty --- becomes the result of the chain. This is exactly the
short-circuiting behavior a pipeline needs.

\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} is also the combinator that makes the
monad laws hold. Left identity (\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} on a
just-constructed value applies the function), right identity
(\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} with a function that returns its input
unchanged is a no-op), and associativity (the order in which
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}s are nested does not affect the result)
all follow from the definition. These laws matter because they let a
reader refactor a chain of \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} calls into
any parenthesization without changing the meaning.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{filter}
Combinator}{The  Combinator}}\label{ch20-the-filter-combinator}

\texttt{filter} keeps the contained value only if a predicate holds;
otherwise it returns an empty \texttt{Maybe}. It does not change the
value's type. The predicate is a function from \texttt{T} to
\texttt{bool}. \texttt{filter} is the combinator for validation: it
inserts a short-circuiting check into a pipeline without breaking the
chain.

The verbatim definition is compact:

\begin{code}
/// Retains value only if predicate passes.
template <typename Pred>
Maybe
filter (Pred &&pred) const
{
  if (value_ && pred (*value_))
    return *this;
  return Maybe{};
}
\end{code}

The return type is \texttt{Maybe}
(i.e.~\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}),
so the type of the pipeline is unchanged by a filter step. When both a
value is present and the predicate accepts it, \texttt{filter} returns
\texttt{*this} --- a copy of the current \texttt{Maybe}. When either
condition fails, it returns a default-constructed (empty)
\texttt{Maybe}.

A validation example keeps only positive integers:

\begin{code}
auto r = dsl::Maybe<int>(-3).filter([](int x){ return x > 0; });
// r is empty
auto s = dsl::Maybe<int>(7).filter([](int x){ return x > 0; });
// s is Maybe<int>(7)
\end{code}

\texttt{filter} composes naturally with \texttt{map}. A pipeline can
transform a value, then validate it, then transform it again, all in one
expression. The first failing filter short-circuits the rest of the
chain to empty. This is the pattern for parsing and validating user
input, where each step has a precondition.

Because \texttt{filter} returns a copy of \texttt{*this} on success, the
value is copied (or moved, depending on \texttt{T}) once per successful
filter. For expensive-to-copy \texttt{T}, the predicate should be cheap
and the number of filter steps small. In practice, filters are usually
applied to scalar or reference-semantics values, where the copy is
negligible.

\section{\texorpdfstring{The \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else}
Member}{The  Member}}\label{ch20-the-or_else-member}

The \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} member serves two roles depending on
context. As the terminal accessor, it collapses a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} to a
\texttt{T} by supplying a fallback. As a recovery step within a
pipeline, it can be used after a chain that may have gone empty to
substitute a default \texttt{Maybe} and continue --- though note that
the member \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} returns a \texttt{T}, not a
\texttt{Maybe}, so pipeline recovery is better expressed by collapsing
and re-wrapping, or by using the free-function
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else} on the
underlying optional (Chapter 21).

The verbatim definition is:

\begin{code}
/// Returns contained value or fallback.
T
or_else (T fallback) const
{
  return value_.value_or (std::move (fallback));
}
\end{code}

The member forwards to
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}value\_\allowbreak{}or},
moving the fallback into the return value when the \texttt{Maybe} is
empty. Because the return type is \texttt{T}, calling
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} ends a \texttt{Maybe} pipeline: the
result is a plain value, not a \texttt{Maybe}, and no further
combinators apply.

The simplest use is defaulting at the end of a chain:

\begin{code}
int port = parse_port(config)
             .filter([](int p){ return p > 0 && p < 65536; })
             .or_else(8080);
\end{code}

If \texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}port} returns empty or the filter rejects
the value, the chain yields \texttt{8080}. Otherwise it yields the
parsed, validated port. The entire validation-and-default logic is one
expression with no branching.

When recovery rather than defaulting is wanted --- that is, when the
pipeline should continue with an alternative \texttt{Maybe} rather than
collapse to a value --- the idiom is to test
\texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and
re-wrap, or to convert to the underlying optional and use the
free-function
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else} from
Chapter 21, which returns an optional rather than a value. The member
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} is deliberately terminal: it forces a
decision, which is usually what the end of a pipeline wants.

\section{Composition: Building a
Pipeline}\label{ch20-composition-building-a-pipeline}

The combinators compose because each returns a \texttt{Maybe}. A
pipeline is a single expression in which \texttt{map},
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}, and \texttt{filter} calls are chained
with the member-call dot. The type of the pipeline may change at each
\texttt{map} or \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} step; it stays constant
through \texttt{filter}. The first step that yields an empty
\texttt{Maybe} short-circuits all subsequent steps to empty.

A canonical example is safe integer parsing. Suppose a function
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}int(\allowbreak{}const std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\&)\allowbreak{}}
returns
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}. A
pipeline that parses, validates, transforms, and defaults looks like
this:

\begin{code}
int n = parse_int(input)
          .filter([](int x){ return x >= 0; })
          .map([](int x){ return x * 2; })
          .or_else(0);
\end{code}

Each step is a pure function. The \texttt{filter} rejects negative
inputs. The \texttt{map} doubles the value. The
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} supplies a default of zero. If
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}int} returns empty, neither the filter nor
the map runs, and \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} returns zero. If the
filter rejects, the map does not run, and \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else}
again returns zero. The chain reads top to bottom as a sequence of
intentions.

A lookup chain that changes types illustrates the combination of
\texttt{map} and \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}. Given a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}Config\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and lookups that return \texttt{Maybe}, the chain fetches a section,
then a value within the section, then transforms the value:

\begin{code}
auto host = load_config()
              .flat_map([](const Config& c){ return c.section("net"); })
              .flat_map([](const Section& s){ return s.value("host"); })
              .map([](const std::string& h){ return normalize_host(h); })
              .or_else(std::string{"localhost"});
\end{code}

The two \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} steps each return a
\texttt{Maybe}, so they are flattened rather than nested. The
\texttt{map} step transforms a value, not a \texttt{Maybe}, so
\texttt{map} is correct there. The terminal
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} collapses the result to a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}.

A validation pipeline that filters and maps in alternation is equally
direct. The pattern is to interleave \texttt{filter} (for checks) and
\texttt{map} (for transformations), ending with
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} or with \texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}value}
at a boundary:

\begin{code}
auto ok = dsl::Maybe<int>(user_input)
            .filter([](int x){ return x >= 1; })
            .filter([](int x){ return x <= 100; })
            .map([](int x){ return x - 1; })
            .filter([](int x){ return x % 2 == 0; });
\end{code}

Each filter is an independent precondition. Multiple filters can be
chained without introducing temporaries, and the chain short-circuits on
the first failure. This is the shape of a parser-validator, and it is
the same shape used internally by MuDSL's parser combinators (Chapter
23) when they gate results.

\section{Value Semantics and
Copy/Move}\label{ch20-value-semantics-and-copymove}

\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is a
value type. Its copy constructor, move constructor, copy assignment, and
move assignment are implicitly defined by the compiler and track those
of
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
When \texttt{T} is copyable,
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
copyable; when \texttt{T} is move-only,
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
move-only. There are no reference members, no shared state, and no
\texttt{virtual} methods.

The combinators are \texttt{const} member functions that return a fresh
\texttt{Maybe} by value. They do not mutate the receiver, so a single
\texttt{Maybe} can be used as the seed for multiple pipelines without
copying concerns beyond the obvious one that each pipeline copies the
value as needed. This is the same discipline as arithmetic:
\texttt{a + b} does not modify \texttt{a}.

\begin{code}
dsl::Maybe<int> seed(10);
auto a = seed.map([](int x){ return x + 1; });
auto b = seed.map([](int x){ return x * 2; });
// seed is still Maybe<int>(10)
\end{code}

Move semantics matter for pipelines that carry move-only types. Because
\texttt{map} constructs its result as
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\{ fn(\allowbreak{}*value\_\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{} \}},
the function receives a \texttt{const T\&} (the dereferenced optional)
and may move from it only if it takes its parameter by value and the
caller passes a movable value. In practice, \texttt{map} on a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}unique\_\allowbreak{}ptr\textless{}\allowbreak{}X\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is uncommon; \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} with a function that takes
ownership is the more usual pattern when moves are required. The
underlying rule is the same as for
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}: the value is not moved
out of a \texttt{const} optional.

The defaulted destructor is also implicit. A
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} with
a non-trivial \texttt{T} destructor will destroy its contained value
when the \texttt{Maybe} goes out of scope, exactly as
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
would. There are no surprises here, which is the point:
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
meant to be a drop-in, value-semantic wrapper that adds combinators
without adding obligations.

\section{The Free-Function
Combinators}\label{ch20-the-free-function-combinators}

Alongside the member combinators, MuDSL provides free functions
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}flat\_\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}filter}, and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else} that
operate on any optional-like value, including raw
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}. These are the subject
of Chapter 21, but they are mentioned here because they are the natural
counterpart to the \texttt{Maybe} members and because the two APIs are
deliberately parallel.

The free functions are templates over the optional type. Their
signatures, quoted verbatim, show the parallel structure:

\begin{code}
template <typename Opt, typename Fn>
auto
map (const Opt &opt, Fn &&fn) -> std::optional<decltype (fn (*opt))>
{
  if (!opt)
    return std::nullopt;
  return fn (*opt);
}

template <typename Opt, typename Fn>
auto
flat_map (const Opt &opt, Fn &&fn) -> decltype (fn (*opt))
{
  using R = decltype (fn (*opt));
  if (!opt)
    return R{};
  return fn (*opt);
}
\end{code}

The free \texttt{map} returns a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}, not a \texttt{Maybe}.
The free \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} returns whatever \texttt{fn}
returns, default-constructing it on emptiness --- the same shape as the
member \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}. The free \texttt{filter} and
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} follow the same pattern. Because
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}as\_\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
returns a
\texttt{const std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\&},
a \texttt{Maybe} can be handed to the free functions directly when a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} result is preferred
over a \texttt{Maybe} result.

The two APIs are interchangeable in intent. The member API is fluent and
is the natural choice when the whole pipeline is expressed in
\texttt{Maybe}. The free API is argument-first and is the natural choice
when the input is already a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} or when generic code
must accept any optional-like type. Mixing the two in a single
expression is possible by routing through
\texttt{as\_\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, though
it is usually clearer to pick one style per pipeline.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Interoperability with
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}}{Interoperability with }}\label{ch20-interoperability-with-stdoptional}

\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
designed to interoperate with
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
without friction. The
\texttt{Maybe(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
constructor lifts an optional into a \texttt{Maybe}; the
\texttt{as\_\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} accessor
exposes the wrapped optional by const reference. Converting in either
direction is a single call.

\begin{code}
std::optional<int> opt = 5;
dsl::Maybe<int> m(opt);
const std::optional<int>& back = m.as_optional();
\end{code}

This bidirectional bridge matters because the standard library is full
of functions that return
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}. Rather than
reimplementing those functions to return \texttt{Maybe}, a pipeline can
wrap their results at the boundary and then use the fluent combinators
internally. The boundary is where the representation changes; inside the
pipeline, everything is a \texttt{Maybe}.

The bridge also makes \texttt{Maybe} a good citizen in generic code. A
template that accepts any optional-like type can be instantiated with
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} as
easily as with
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
provided it only uses operations common to both (such as
\texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}value} and dereference). For the free-function
combinators, \texttt{Maybe} works directly because
\texttt{as\_\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns a
reference to a real optional and the free functions only require
\texttt{operator*} and \texttt{operator bool}.

When a \texttt{Maybe} must be returned from a function that the standard
library expects to produce an
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional}, the conversion is
equally direct: construct a \texttt{Maybe} internally, then return
\texttt{m.\allowbreak{}as\_\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
(or rely on the implicit conversion path through the optional
constructor). There is no need to choose between the fluent style and
standard-library compatibility; both are available at the cost of a
single constructor call.

\section{Worked Example: Safe Integer
Parsing}\label{ch20-worked-example-safe-integer-parsing}

Consider the task of parsing an integer from a string, clamping it to a
valid range, and converting it to an enum. Each step can fail: the
string may not be a number, the number may be out of range, and the
number may not correspond to any enumerator. A \texttt{Maybe} pipeline
expresses this directly.

\begin{code}
enum class Level { Low, Medium, High };

dsl::Maybe<int> parse_int(const std::string&);       // returns empty on failure

dsl::Maybe<Level> to_level(int x) {
  switch (x) {
    case 0: return dsl::Maybe<Level>{Level::Low};
    case 1: return dsl::Maybe<Level>{Level::Medium};
    case 2: return dsl::Maybe<Level>{Level::High};
    default: return dsl::Maybe<Level>{};
  }
}

Level resolve(const std::string& input) {
  return parse_int(input)
           .filter([](int x){ return x >= 0 && x <= 2; })
           .flat_map(to_level)
           .or_else(Level::Low);
}
\end{code}

The pipeline reads as a specification: parse, validate the range,
convert to an enum, default to \texttt{Low}. The
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} is necessary because
\texttt{to\_\allowbreak{}level} itself returns a \texttt{Maybe}; the
range filter makes the second failure impossible in practice, but the
type system still requires \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} because
\texttt{to\_\allowbreak{}level}'s return type is
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}Level\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
not \texttt{Level}.

If the filter were omitted, \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} would still
handle an out-of-range input correctly: \texttt{to\_\allowbreak{}level}
would return an empty \texttt{Maybe}, \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}
would flatten it to empty, and \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} would
supply \texttt{Level:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Low}. The filter is
present not for correctness but for clarity --- it documents the
expected range as an explicit step in the chain. This is a common idiom:
filters are used as much for readability as for runtime behavior.

The example also shows the role of \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} as a
terminator. The pipeline produces a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}Level\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
and
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else(\allowbreak{}Level:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Low)\allowbreak{}}
collapses it to a \texttt{Level}. After \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else},
no further \texttt{Maybe} combinators are available, which is correct:
the function has decided on a value, and the pipeline is over.

\section{Worked Example: A Lookup
Chain}\label{ch20-worked-example-a-lookup-chain}

A second example is a multi-level lookup. A configuration store maps
names to sections, and each section maps keys to string values. Both
lookups may fail. The chain fetches a section, then a value, then trims
whitespace.

\begin{code}
dsl::Maybe<const Section&> find_section(const Store&, const std::string&);
dsl::Maybe<std::string> find_value(const Section&, const std::string&);
std::string trim(const std::string&);

std::string lookup(const Store& store) {
  return find_section(store, "net")
           .flat_map([](const Section& s){ return find_value(s, "host"); })
           .map([](const std::string& v){ return trim(v); })
           .or_else(std::string{"localhost"});
}
\end{code}

The two lookups both return \texttt{Maybe}, so they are chained with
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}. The \texttt{trim} step is a pure
transformation, so \texttt{map} is correct. The terminal
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} supplies a default. If either lookup
fails, the chain short-circuits to empty and the default is returned.

This shape scales to any depth. A three-level lookup --- store to
section, section to key, key to typed value --- adds one more
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}. The type of the chain changes at each
step, but the structure is uniform: \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} for
fallible steps, \texttt{map} for infallible transformations,
\texttt{filter} for checks, \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} to terminate.

The example also illustrates the difference between \texttt{map} and
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} concretely. Had \texttt{trim} been
wrapped in a function returning
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(say, to signal that trimming could fail on malformed input), the chain
would need \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} for that step too. The choice
of combinator is dictated entirely by the return type of the step's
function: plain value means \texttt{map}, \texttt{Maybe} means
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Worked Example: Validation with \texttt{filter}
and
\texttt{map}}{Worked Example: Validation with  and }}\label{ch20-worked-example-validation-with-filter-and-map}

A validation pipeline interposes checks between transformations. Suppose
an input is read as a string, parsed to an integer, checked for range,
scaled, and checked again for a downstream constraint. Each check is a
\texttt{filter}; each transformation is a \texttt{map}.

\begin{code}
dsl::Maybe<int> read_int(const std::string&);

auto result = read_int(raw)
                .filter([](int x){ return x > 0; })
                .map([](int x){ return x * 10; })
                .filter([](int x){ return x < 1000; })
                .map([](int x){ return x + 1; });
\end{code}

The pipeline expresses a sequence of preconditions and transformations.
The first failing \texttt{filter} short-circuits everything after it.
The final \texttt{result} is a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}};
the caller can \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} it to a default or test
\texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}value} and report a validation error.

Interleaving \texttt{filter} and \texttt{map} is the idiomatic way to
express multi-step validation. An equivalent implementation with
\texttt{if} statements would require a temporary variable at each step
and a nested branch for each failure. The \texttt{Maybe} chain collapses
all of that into one expression, with the short-circuit semantics
provided by the combinators rather than by hand-written control flow.

A subtle point: the order of \texttt{filter} and \texttt{map} matters.
Filtering before mapping is cheaper when the filter is likely to reject,
because the map is skipped. Filtering after mapping is necessary when
the filter depends on the mapped value. In the example above, the second
\texttt{filter} depends on the scaled value, so it must come after the
\texttt{map}. The pipeline structure makes this ordering explicit and
visible.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Pitfalls: \texttt{map} Versus
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}}{Pitfalls:  Versus }}\label{ch20-pitfalls-map-versus-flat_map}

The most common mistake with
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
using \texttt{map} where \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} is required. If
the function passed to \texttt{map} returns a \texttt{Maybe}, the result
is a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
--- a nested optional --- rather than the flattened
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} the
caller almost certainly wants. The type system does not prevent this,
because
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}U\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is a legal type.

\begin{code}
dsl::Maybe<int> find(int);
auto bad  = dsl::Maybe<int>(1).map(find);       // Maybe<Maybe<int>>
auto good = dsl::Maybe<int>(1).flat_map(find);  // Maybe<int>
\end{code}

The \texttt{bad} chain is not incorrect --- it type-checks and runs ---
but it is almost never what was intended. A subsequent \texttt{map} on
\texttt{bad} would operate on the outer \texttt{Maybe}, passing a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}int\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} to
the next function, which is unlikely to match the function's expected
\texttt{int} parameter. The mistake surfaces as a type error one or two
steps downstream, which can be confusing to diagnose.

The rule is simple: if the function returns a \texttt{Maybe}, use
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}; if it returns a plain value, use
\texttt{map}. The function's return type, not its name, determines the
combinator. Naming a function \texttt{find} does not make it require
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}; returning a \texttt{Maybe} does.

A related pitfall is passing a function that takes
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} to
\texttt{map}. The \texttt{map} combinator passes the contained
\texttt{T} to the function, not the
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}. A
function written to accept
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} will
either fail to compile (if
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
not constructible from \texttt{T}) or will be called with a \texttt{T}
and silently misbehave. The function passed to \texttt{map} must accept
\texttt{T} (or a type \texttt{T} converts to).

\section{Pitfalls: Empty Propagation}\label{ch20-pitfalls-empty-propagation}

Empty propagation is the feature, not a bug, but it has a pitfall of its
own: it is silent. A \texttt{Maybe} pipeline that goes empty produces no
output, no error, and no indication of which step failed. For
transformations where the absence is the expected negative result, this
is ideal. For diagnostics, it is inadequate.

When diagnostics are required, the right tool is
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 22), which carries an error value through the chain in the same
way \texttt{Maybe} carries emptiness. \texttt{Maybe} is for cases where
the absence is self-explanatory: a missing lookup, an unmatched pattern,
an optional configuration value. \texttt{Result} is for cases where the
caller needs to know why the step failed.

A second aspect of empty propagation is that \texttt{map} and
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} do not call their functions on an empty
\texttt{Maybe}. This is the property that makes them safe to chain, but
it means side effects placed in a \texttt{map} lambda will not run if
the input is empty. A \texttt{map} step that logs the value, for
instance, will silently skip the log when the input is absent. Side
effects in combinators are best avoided; when they are necessary, they
should be placed at the end of the pipeline, after
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} or after an explicit
\texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}value} check.

A third aspect is the interaction between \texttt{filter} and emptiness.
A \texttt{filter} on an empty \texttt{Maybe} returns an empty
\texttt{Maybe} without calling the predicate. This is correct --- there
is no value to test --- but it means a predicate that assumes a value is
present will not be exercised on the empty path. Predicates should be
pure functions of \texttt{T} and should not depend on having been
called.

\section{Pitfalls: Purity and the Monad
Laws}\label{ch20-pitfalls-purity-and-the-monad-laws}

The documentation for
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
states that the monad laws hold when the functions passed to
\texttt{map} and \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} are pure and
side-effect free. This caveat is standard but worth elaborating. The
laws --- left identity, right identity, and associativity --- are
properties of the algebra, not of the implementation. The implementation
upholds them mechanically; impure functions violate them by side effect.

Left identity says that constructing a \texttt{Maybe} from a value and
then \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}ping a function is equivalent to
calling the function directly on the value. Right identity says that
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} with a function that wraps its argument
in a \texttt{Maybe} is equivalent to the original \texttt{Maybe}.
Associativity says that the grouping of \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}
calls does not affect the result. All three hold for \texttt{Maybe} as
defined, provided the functions do not mutate shared state or depend on
mutable globals.

When a function passed to \texttt{map} or
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} has side effects --- logging, mutation,
I/O --- the laws can appear to fail because the side effects occur a
different number of times or in a different order than a reader expects.
The fix is not to change the combinators but to extract the side effects
from the functions. Pure transformations compose; side-effecting
procedures do not. This is the same discipline required for any
functional pipeline, including the memoized functions of Chapter 18 and
the lazy values of Chapter 19.

In practice, MuDSL pipelines that use \texttt{Maybe} are almost always
pure. The type is small enough that the temptation to embed side effects
is low, and the fluent style encourages a separation between
transformation (in the chain) and observation (at the chain's end). When
this separation is maintained, the monad laws hold and the chain can be
refactored with confidence.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Relationship to
\texttt{Lazy\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} and
Memoization}{Relationship to  and Memoization}}\label{ch20-relationship-to-lazyt-and-memoization}

\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
related to, but distinct from, the laziness utilities of Chapters 18 and
19. \texttt{Lazy\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 19) defers a single computation until its result is demanded,
then caches it.
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
represents a value that may be absent; it is not deferred and does not
cache. A
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}Lazy\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is a deferred computation that may turn out to be unavailable, and a
\texttt{Lazy\textless{}\allowbreak{}Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is a computation that, when forced, may yield no value --- both are
legal and occasionally useful.

Memoization (Chapter 18) caches the result of a pure function keyed by
its arguments. A memoized function that returns
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
the natural way to cache lookups that may fail: the cache stores both
presence and absence, so repeated lookups for a missing key do not
recompute. The \texttt{Maybe} returned by such a memoized function
composes directly into the pipelines described in this chapter.

The three utilities are thus complementary. \texttt{Lazy} defers,
\texttt{Maybe} models absence, and memoization caches. A pipeline that
fetches a configuration value lazily, validates it with \texttt{filter},
transforms it with \texttt{map}, and defaults it with
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} uses all three. The boundary between them
is clean: each utility has a single responsibility, and they connect
through the common currency of values.

\section{\texorpdfstring{Relationship to
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}}{Relationship to }}\label{ch20-relationship-to-resultte}

\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 22) is the error-carrying counterpart of
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Where \texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
has two states --- present and empty ---
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
has two states --- ok and err --- with the err state carrying an error
value of type \texttt{E}. The two types share the same combinator
vocabulary: \texttt{map}, \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} (often called
\texttt{and\_\allowbreak{}then} on \texttt{Result}), \texttt{filter},
and \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else}.

The choice between them is a question of information. If the caller of a
function needs to know why it failed,
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
is the right type: the error is carried through the chain and can be
inspected at the end. If the caller only needs to know that it failed,
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
simpler and carries less noise. A lookup in a static map, for instance,
typically returns \texttt{Maybe}: there is no interesting error to
report. A parse, by contrast, often returns \texttt{Result}: the
position and nature of the parse failure are useful to the caller.

The two types are not interchangeable in a single pipeline without
conversion. A
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} can
be lifted into a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
by supplying a default error, and a
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
can be collapsed to a
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} by
discarding the error. The conversion is a one-liner in either direction,
but it forces a decision: at the point of conversion, information is
either added (a default error) or discarded (the error value). Pipelines
that mix the two should convert at a well-defined boundary rather than
ad hoc.

In MuDSL's parser infrastructure (Chapters 23 through 25),
\texttt{Result}-like types dominate because parser failures carry
diagnostic information. \texttt{Maybe} appears in the higher-level
orchestration --- caching lookups, optional configuration, optional
rewrites --- where the absence is self-explanatory. The two coexist
comfortably, each used where its information content matches the
problem.

\section{Stylistic Notes}\label{ch20-stylistic-notes}

A few conventions make \texttt{Maybe} pipelines easier to read and
maintain. First, prefer one combinator per line, with the dot at the
start of the next line, so that the pipeline reads as a vertical list of
steps. This mirrors the layout used in the examples above and is the
same style used for parser combinators in Chapter 23.

Second, name the step functions when a lambda would be opaque. A lambda
like
\texttt{[](\allowbreak{}int x)\allowbreak{}\{ return x * 2; \allowbreak{}\}}
is clear enough, but
\texttt{[](\allowbreak{}const Section\& s\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}\{ return s.\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{}"host")\allowbreak{}; \}}
is often better expressed as a named function
\texttt{host\_\allowbreak{}of}. Named functions document intent, are
reusable, and are easier to test in isolation. The pipeline then reads
as a composition of named operations, which is closer to a
specification.

Third, terminate pipelines explicitly. A pipeline that ends in
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} has decided on a value. A pipeline that
ends in a \texttt{Maybe} has not; the caller must handle the empty case.
Ending in \texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}value} at a boundary makes the
decision point visible. Ending in an un-inspected \texttt{Maybe} is a
smell, because the emptiness is being propagated without being
addressed.

Fourth, keep pipelines pure. Side effects in combinators break the monad
laws and make the chain hard to reason about. If a side effect is
required --- logging, instrumentation --- perform it at the end of the
pipeline, on a value that has already been collapsed, or in a separate
pass. The combinators are for transformation; observation is a separate
concern.

Finally, prefer \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} to nested \texttt{map}
when nesting would occur. A \texttt{map} returning a \texttt{Maybe} is a
sign that \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map} was intended. Reading the
chain left to right, each step's function should return a plain value
for \texttt{map} or a \texttt{Maybe} for
\texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}; a chain that mixes the two should do so
deliberately, not accidentally.

\section{Summary}\label{ch20-summary}

\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is
MuDSL's monadic optional: a thin value wrapper around
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
with fluent \texttt{map}, \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{filter}, and \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} combinators. It is
constructed empty as
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}\{\}},
with a value as
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}x)\allowbreak{}},
or from an existing optional as
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}opt)\allowbreak{}}.
Access is through
\texttt{has\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{as\_\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, and the
terminal
\texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else(\allowbreak{}fallback)\allowbreak{}}.

\texttt{map} applies a pure function to the contained value, changing
the type as the function dictates. \texttt{flat\_\allowbreak{}map}
applies a function that returns a \texttt{Maybe} and flattens the
result, which is the combinator for chaining fallible steps.
\texttt{filter} retains the value only if a predicate holds, providing
short-circuiting validation. \texttt{or\_\allowbreak{}else} collapses a
\texttt{Maybe} to a plain value by supplying a fallback.

The combinators compose into pipelines that short-circuit to empty on
the first failure, expressing validation, lookup, and transformation
logic as a single fluent expression without branching noise.
\texttt{Maybe\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} is a
value type with the same copy and move semantics as
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}},
and it interoperates with
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} through the optional
constructor and the
\texttt{as\_\allowbreak{}optional(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} accessor.

The free-function combinators
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}flat\_\allowbreak{}map},
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}filter}, and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}else} (Chapter
21) provide the same operations on raw
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} and on any
optional-like type, and are the natural counterpart to the
\texttt{Maybe} members. For error-carrying flows,
\texttt{Result\textless{}\allowbreak{}T,\allowbreak{}E\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 22) extends the same combinator vocabulary with an explicit
error channel. For deferred computations,
\texttt{Lazy\textless{}\allowbreak{}T\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
(Chapter 19) and the memoization utilities of Chapter 18 complement
\texttt{Maybe} without overlapping it.
